#: src/CoreOptions.php:271
msgid "Use CrawlCache"
msgstr ""

#: src/CoreOptions.php:275
msgid "Skip crawling unchanged URLs."
msgstr ""

#: src/CoreOptions.php:286
msgid "Follow links while crawling"
msgstr ""

#: src/CoreOptions.php:290
msgid "Also crawl URLs found in the pages as they are crawled, not only the ones detection produced. It reads img src and srcset, video, audio, source, link and script, so it is what brings the media a page shows into the published site, and it finds pages reachable by a link and nothing else — a hand-written link in a post, a route a plugin renders. On by default: with \"Detect Uploads\" off, this is what keeps the images in the site."
msgstr ""

#: src/CoreOptions.php:301
msgid "Snipcart public API key"
msgstr ""

#: src/CoreOptions.php:305
msgid "For a WooCommerce shop being published as a static site: with a key here, the add-to-cart buttons are rewritten for Snipcart, which is a cart that runs in the browser. WooCommerce's own cart is PHP and cannot be published. Empty means no page is touched. Shown only where WooCommerce is active."
msgstr ""

#: src/CoreOptions.php:314
msgid "Form Endpoint"
msgstr ""

#: src/CoreOptions.php:318
msgid "Where the published forms should post. A static site cannot run PHP, so a form left pointing at WordPress is a button that does nothing; give it the URL of a form service — Formspree, Basin, Web3Forms — and it will work. Empty means no form is touched at all."
msgstr ""

#: src/CoreOptions.php:327
msgid "Form Handling"
msgstr ""

#: src/CoreOptions.php:331
msgid "\"endpoint\" points forms at the URL above. \"netlify\" instead marks them for Netlify Forms, which is not a URL but two marks on the markup — use it only when the site is deployed to Netlify."
msgstr ""

#: src/CoreOptions.php:340
msgid "Form Rules"
msgstr ""

#: src/CoreOptions.php:344
msgid "One rule per line, as \"id = endpoint\", where the id is the form's id or name attribute. It overrides the endpoint above for that one form. Leave the endpoint empty — \"id =\" — to leave that form exactly as it is."
msgstr ""

#: src/CoreOptions.php:354
msgid "Additional Paths to Crawl"
msgstr ""

#: src/CoreOptions.php:358
msgid "One path per line, for what nothing enumerates and nothing links to: a file put on the server by hand, a route a plugin answers without a post behind it. They are added to detection, not to the crawl queue directly — anything the detection does not name is removed from the queue on the next run."
msgstr ""

#: src/CoreOptions.php:368
msgid "Crawl Chunk Size"
msgstr ""

#: src/CoreOptions.php:372
msgid "How many URLs to hold in memory at a time. 0 takes the whole queue at once, which is what it has always done and is fine for most sites; set a few thousand if the crawl runs out of memory on a very large one."
msgstr ""

#: src/CoreOptions.php:381
msgid "Crawl Progress Interval"
msgstr ""

#: src/CoreOptions.php:385
msgid "Write a progress line to the log every this many URLs. 0 turns the running count off and leaves only the line at the end."
msgstr ""

#: src/CoreOptions.php:394
msgid "Detect Redirection plugin URLs"
msgstr ""

#: src/CoreOptions.php:398
msgid "Crawl the source addresses of the redirects the Redirection plugin manages, so the 301s they answer with end up in the static site. Off where that plugin is not installed: it does nothing."
msgstr ""

#: src/CoreOptions.php:407
msgid "Completion Email"
msgstr ""

#: src/CoreOptions.php:411
msgid "Email to send deployment completion notification to."
msgstr ""

#: src/CoreOptions.php:420
msgid "Completion Webhook"
msgstr ""

#: src/CoreOptions.php:424
